From 4eaacf991ac815ccde38210dc0cecfb2630160fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:05:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: vop2: Plan the video PLL in a private atomic
 object

The video PLL plan of atomic_check still depends on the hardware state
and on what the commits before set:

- It rounds the CRU rates with the PLL at its current rate, which may be
  a leftover of a video port that no longer uses it, and reads the PLL
  rate and its rate protection, both of which a nonblocking commit that
  has not run yet is about to change.
- A video port that keeps running from the PLL pins its rate, so that
  an HDMI mode that needs another PLL rate is refused until the other
  port is switched off, while the same pair of modes set from all
  outputs off is accepted.
- The CRU may still pick another parent than the one checked when the
  DCLK is set, as it reparents to the closest rate.
- A DisplayPort mode that a fixed PLL gives within the tolerance can
  take a free PLL instead, and then block an HDMI mode that needs it.

Plan the DCLK of all video ports from the new state only, in
mode_config.atomic_check, with the PLL in a private atomic object that
holds its rate and the video ports that use it:

- HDMI runs from the HDMI PHY PLL when that can make the pixel clock
  (TMDS); otherwise a video port runs from a fixed PLL (GPLL, CPLL,
  AUPLL) if that gives its DCLK exactly or, for outputs other than HDMI,
  within the pixel clock tolerance of 0.5 %. All others share the video
  PLL, at the rate that serves all of them (HDMI exactly), or the state
  is refused.
- The PLL rate only depends on the DCLKs of its users, not on its
  current rate or on the order in which the outputs are set, so the
  same state is accepted or refused whatever came before it.
- If the rate changes while a user keeps running, that CRTC is added to
  the state with a modeset and the state is checked again; that needs
  DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_ALLOW_MODESET (a commit that changes the users of the
  PLL is a modeset already).
- The CRTC state records the chosen parent; atomic_enable selects it on
  the CRU divider (named in the device tree, as the parents are) and
  sets the rate. The commit disables video ports before it enables
  any, so the first video port enabled from the PLL sets its rate once,
  with no other port running from it.
- The video ports hold one exclusive rate protection on the PLL while
  any of them runs from it, so that no other consumer changes it.
- The CRU dividers have 7 bits on video ports 0 and 3, 5 bits on video
  ports 1 and 2.

Without the new clocks in the device tree (and on other SoCs) the DCLK
is checked against the CRU as before.

Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
